<Layout
  title="The Plough and the Guarantee — Shaurya Vijay Sinha"
  description="A Bastiat-and-Kautilya reading of CGTMSE's flat guarantee fee, projecting the fiscal cost of underpriced risk through FY30."
>
  <article class="section section--tight">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Risk pricing · NFPRC research</p>
      <h1>The Plough and the Guarantee</h1>
      <p style="font-size:1.05rem; font-style:italic; color:var(--body-text); margin-top:-0.4em;">
        What CGTMSE's near-uniform guarantee fee costs, read through Frédéric
        Bastiat's <em>Essays on Political Economy</em> and Kautilya's <em>Arthashastra</em>.
      </p>

      <div class="key-finding">
        <p class="key-finding__label">Key finding, from the accompanying projection model</p>
        <p class="key-finding__figure">Rs 15.02 lakh crore unpriced gap by FY30</p>
        <p class="key-finding__context">
          Projected forward from a Rs 9 lakh crore FY25 base, flat-rate pricing
          (35% historical CAGR) reaches Rs 40.36 lakh crore in cumulative
          exposure by FY30, against Rs 25.34 lakh crore under a risk-tiered
          alternative (23% CAGR). The gap between them — Rs 15.02 lakh crore —
          is 37% of total flat-rate exposure: guarantee risk the fee structure
          never actually prices.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>One plough, two farmers</h2>
      <p>
        Bastiat's <em>That Which Is Seen, and That Which Is Not Seen</em>
        imagines a country with exactly one plough. Two farmers want it. Peter,
        who owns it, is inclined to lend it to John, who has built a
        reputation for repayment. James has none. The state steps in and
        guarantees James's loan, so Peter lends to James instead. What's easy
        to see is that James now has a plough. What's easy to miss is that the
        plough didn't come from new resources — it was reallocated from John,
        who loses out twice: once to James, and again as a taxpayer funding a
        choice he didn't make.
      </p>
      <p>
        The loanable-funds critique this sets up is straightforward: within a
        given period, the capital available for lending is fixed. A guarantee
        scheme doesn't create more of it — it shifts who gets to use what
        already exists. Whether that's a good trade depends entirely on
        whether James makes better use of the plough than John would have —
        a question Bastiat's parable never actually tests, because in his
        example James isn't shown to benefit at all. That gap is exactly where
        forty years of development economics has since built an entire
        literature — and where the parable starts to come apart.
      </p>

      <h2>CGTMSE, at scale</h2>
      <p>
        The Credit Guarantee Fund Trust for Micro and Small Enterprises
        (CGTMSE) exists because many viable small businesses lack the
        collateral banks require. The Trust covers up to 85% of a defaulted
        loan, letting banks lend without it. The scheme has grown fast — from
        roughly Rs 5 lakh crore in cumulative guarantees in October 2023 to a
        modelled Rs 9 lakh crore base by FY25 — and growth this fast makes one
        question harder to avoid: is the risk actually priced correctly?
      </p>
      <p>
        The annual guarantee fee is close to flat — around 1% of the
        outstanding loan, with modest reductions for smaller loans and
        women-led enterprises. A textile manufacturer in Bengal with years of
        stable cash flow ends up paying close to the same fee as a first-time
        entrepreneur with no track record. When a near-flat fee is applied to a
        highly variable-risk pool, safer borrowers are quietly cross-subsidising
        riskier ones — and the guarantee fund, ultimately the taxpayer, absorbs
        the difference instead of it showing up in what any single borrower
        pays.
      </p>

      <div class="exhibit">
        <img src="/images/cgtmse-chart-1.png" alt="Line chart projecting CGTMSE's cumulative guarantee exposure from FY25 to FY30 under flat-rate pricing versus risk-tiered pricing, with the flat-rate line diverging well above the risk-tiered line and a leverage-ceiling reference line held nearly flat near the bottom." loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Exhibit — flat-rate exposure reaches Rs 40.36 lakh crore by FY30 against Rs 25.34 lakh crore under risk-tiered pricing; both sit far above CGTMSE's own board-approved 10x leverage ceiling on its Rs 9,000 crore corpus.</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h2>Kautilya's answer, two thousand years earlier</h2>
      <p>
        Bastiat's defence of interest — that it compensates a lender's
        patience as much as the capital itself — was written to counter a
        European debate that Indian commercial thought had already moved past.
        The Christian church spent roughly a millennium treating interest as
        sin, full stop. Kautilya's <em>Arthashastra</em>, written some four
        centuries before Christ, never entertained that question. It priced
        interest by risk instead: around 15% for ordinary commercial loans,
        higher for riskier forest trade, highest of all for maritime trade,
        where a single storm could sink the entire principal. That is a
        risk-adjusted rate ceiling set sector by sector — the same exercise a
        modern guarantee architect is attempting, and sometimes failing, to
        perform today.
      </p>
      <p>
        Applied to CGTMSE, the Kautilyan answer isn't that government has no
        business in credit markets — Kautilya assumed it did. It's that
        intervention has to track risk, not flatten it. A scheme can widen
        access to credit and still fail if it stops distinguishing between the
        borrower who merely lacks collateral and the one who is genuinely
        likely to default.
      </p>

      <h2>What the gap actually means</h2>
      <p>
        CGTMSE's own recent reforms — risk-sensitive fee cuts for ZED-certified
        firms, reduced cover for distressed accounts, tighter rules against
        guaranteeing already-defaulted loans — move in a risk-based direction,
        but the fee structure has changed far more slowly than the guarantee
        ceilings have expanded. The projection above is what that lag costs:
        not a prediction that the scheme fails, but a measure of how much
        exposure accumulates while pricing catches up. A 2020 CAG audit found
        CGTMSE's actual leverage against its corpus reached as high as 64
        times in some years, against a board-approved ceiling of 10x — this
        model's projected exposure is already running at a similar multiple of
        that ceiling by FY30, under either pricing path.
      </p>
